														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3557168">
									

<p>It's a &quot;gap&quot; in time, so things happened before, and things happened after, but not during. There are fossils and rocks from the Permian, and fossils and rocks from the Triassic. There aren't any that date from the intervening period. As for the stars - when you look at a galaxy 250 million light years away, you're seeing light that was emitted 250 million light years ago. Since there was &quot;nothing&quot; there 252 million years ago, you can't see anything, but go further and the galaxies are visible because the event hasn't happened for them yet.</p>
<p>In real life, we believe that there was a rise in temperature and increase in UV radiation during that period, based largely on fossil records, and that's the same starting process here. Study of prehistory can consist of a lot of educated guesses and inferences, trying to paint a picture based on very scarce evidence. If anything, the Foundation scientists have evidence that is a lot more concrete and substantial than real world geologists and paleontologists do, and STILL need to add magical SCP confirmation because, well, reality is less plausible than fiction.</p>

							</div>

								
