					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/theshrike" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1834351); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1834351&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606509373" alt="TheShrike" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1834351)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/theshrike" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1834351); return false;" >TheShrike</a></span> <span class="odate time_1500957167 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">25 Jul 2017 04:32</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3557267">
									

<p>So, the thing is, this doesn't imply that time didn't exist&#8230; Just that certain things weren't happening during that time.</p>
<p>For us to not see the stars in that distance range, but see the ones before and after, they must have not been producing light&#8230; But, light must have kept moving. Otherwise, we'd still see those stars' light, since it was frozen, and then started moving again.</p>
<p>And for the fossils not to be dated to that period, no fossils could have been created at the time&#8230; But, geological processes must have kept going. Otherwise the rock &quot;older than that time period&quot; would just look younger and there'd be no apparent problem.</p>
<p>Basically, for us to notice missing time, there must be something to compare to that's&#8230; Outside time, I guess. And you have provided things to compare to, but they're within time.</p>
<p>So, unless you come up with some different way for the Foundation to notice this, your current setup describes a time period during which some thinhs, but not all things, stopped happening.</p>

							</div>

								
					
